---
import BlogArticle from '../../components/BlogArticle.astro';
import BlogCode from '../../components/BlogCode.astro';

const contents = [
  { id: 'gap', label: 'What plan can\'t tell you' },
  { id: 'mode', label: 'Turn on Terraform mode' },
  { id: 'credentials', label: 'Give the provider credentials it accepts' },
  { id: 'apply', label: 'Plan and apply against localhost' },
  { id: 'routing', label: 'When a resource ignores the endpoint' },
];
const tfConfig = `# localcloud.yaml
version: 1
host:
  environment:
    LOCALCLOUD_TERRAFORM_MODE: "true"`;
const tfCommands = `localcloud start --local-only
eval "$(localcloud env --format terraform)"
export GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS="$PWD/.localcloud/fake-service-account.json"
curl -fsS "http://localhost:5380/terraform/readiness?mode=endpoint"

terraform init
terraform plan
terraform apply -auto-approve`;
---

<BlogArticle
  slug="validate-gcp-terraform-locally"
  kind="Walkthrough"
  deck="terraform plan tells you what Terraform intends to do. It can't tell you what the API will say when it tries. Run the apply against a local Google Cloud first and find out before a shared project is left half-built."
  primary={{ href: '/docs/terraform/', label: 'Terraform setup guide' }}
  secondary={{ href: '/docs/', label: 'Quick start' }}
  {contents}
  outcome="A terraform apply of your Google provider resources that completes against localhost, using a local state backend and credentials that are worthless anywhere else."
  needs="Terraform with the hashicorp/google provider, version 7"
  next={{ posts: ['zero-secret-gcp-ci-github-actions', 'zero-ticket-cloud-prototyping'], doc: { href: '/docs/terraform/', label: 'Terraform guide: routing modes, credentials and readiness' } }}
>
  <section>
    <h2 id="gap">What plan can't tell you</h2>
    <p class="mt-4">A clean plan means the configuration is valid and the provider knows how to turn it into API calls. Whether those calls succeed is decided at apply time, by the API: a subscription that references a topic created in the wrong order, an attribute the service rejects, a module output that is empty when the next resource needs it.</p>
    <p class="mt-4">Those failures arrive halfway through an apply in a shared project. Some resources exist, some don't, and someone has to work out which before anybody else can deploy.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="mode">Turn on Terraform mode</h2>
    <p class="mt-4">Save this next to your Terraform code before you start the runtime. It stops LocalCloud loading its sample data, so everything in the project is something Terraform created.</p>
    <BlogCode label="localcloud.yaml" code={tfConfig} copy="copy-blog-tf-yaml" />
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="credentials">Give the provider credentials it accepts</h2>
    <p class="mt-4">Version 7 of the Google provider parses its credentials file, so an empty file won't do. It needs a well-formed service-account JSON with a real RSA key in it. The <a href="/docs/terraform/#create-valid-fake-credentials">Terraform guide</a> has a short script that generates one whose token URL points at localhost. It grants nothing on Google Cloud; keep it out of version control anyway.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="apply">Plan and apply against localhost</h2>
    <BlogCode label="Run Terraform against LocalCloud" code={tfCommands} copy="copy-blog-tf-cmd" />
    <p class="mt-4"><code>localcloud env --format terraform</code> exports the custom endpoints the provider reads. The readiness request confirms the runtime is set up for Terraform before you spend time on a plan. Use a local state backend for this, never the one your real environments share.</p>
    <p class="mt-4">When the apply finishes, the resources are there to inspect: open the web console, or point your application at the same runtime and run its tests against infrastructure your module just built. Then change the module and apply again. A full destroy and recreate costs seconds.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="routing">When a resource ignores the endpoint</h2>
    <p class="mt-4">Most provider clients honor custom endpoints, which is all the commands above rely on. A few, BigQuery among them, call fixed hostnames. For those, LocalCloud has an opt-in <a href="/docs/terraform/#transparent-network">transparent networking</a> mode that routes the provider's requests with local DNS and TLS. Start with endpoint mode and switch only if a resource you need requires it.</p>
    <p class="mt-4">A local apply proves the module's structure and ordering. Your staging plan is still where you review the diff against real state, and it should now hold fewer surprises.</p>
  </section>
</BlogArticle>
